1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,466
let me ask you something what do you think is the most important skill

2
00:00:03,466 --> 00:00:06,200
your child will need in the next 10 years

3
00:00:06,633 --> 00:00:09,266
coding mathematics

4
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:11,066
a second language

5
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:15,266
those are good answers but they are not the right answer

6
00:00:15,833 --> 00:00:19,366
because here is what the data from over 1,000 employers

7
00:00:19,366 --> 00:00:22,533
representing 14 million workers actually tells us

8
00:00:23,066 --> 00:00:27,233
The World Economic Forum's Future of Jobs Report 2025

9
00:00:27,233 --> 00:00:32,900
projects that 39% of core workplace skills will change by 2030

10
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,566
not some skills nearly four in every 10 skills that are valued today

11
00:00:37,666 --> 00:00:41,400
will either disappear or become less important within this decade

12
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:45,400
and 59% of the global workforce will need to be reskilled

13
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:49,300
that includes the people teaching your children right now

14
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:52,266
so the question is not just what jobs are coming

15
00:00:52,766 --> 00:00:55,800
the question is what kind of human being do you need to raise

16
00:00:56,100 --> 00:01:00,566
to thrive in a world being reshaped by artificial intelligence

17
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:05,533
the answer is both simpler and more demanding than most parents expect

18
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:08,966
your child needs two things working together

19
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:12,900
technical fluency and deep human capability

20
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:15,500
not one or the other

21
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:21,366
both woven together into what researchers call a t shape profile

22
00:01:21,766 --> 00:01:24,200
deep expertise in at least one area

23
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:28,800
combined with broad skills that cross discipline and adapt to change

24
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:31,400
let us start with the technical side

25
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:36,300
the No. 1 fastest growing skill on the World Economic Forum list

26
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:41,066
is not so coding it is artificial intelligence literacy

27
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:43,866
understanding what artificial intelligence is

28
00:01:43,866 --> 00:01:46,700
how it works where it fails

29
00:01:46,866 --> 00:01:50,866
and when to trust it a child who cannot critically

30
00:01:51,066 --> 00:01:54,000
evaluate an artificial intelligence output

31
00:01:54,166 --> 00:01:56,600
is not prepared for the world that is coming

32
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:58,400
they are vulnerable to it

33
00:01:58,433 --> 00:02:02,900
the second critical technical skill is computational thinking

34
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:05,366
not memorizing programming syntax

35
00:02:05,366 --> 00:02:09,500
but learning to break complex problem into smaller parts

36
00:02:09,800 --> 00:02:13,333
recognize patterns and design step by step solutions

37
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:17,866
this is the mental framework that makes someone effective

38
00:02:17,866 --> 00:02:20,666
whether they are using artificial intelligence tools

39
00:02:20,900 --> 00:02:24,066
managing a team or launching a business

40
00:02:24,066 --> 00:02:25,600
third is data literacy

41
00:02:25,700 --> 00:02:29,400
we live in a world where artificial intelligence systems

42
00:02:29,466 --> 00:02:32,066
are built entirely on data

43
00:02:32,300 --> 00:02:34,600
child who can read interpret

44
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:37,100
question and challenge data

45
00:02:37,100 --> 00:02:38,800
is a child who can understand

46
00:02:38,866 --> 00:02:40,666
the world more clearly

47
00:02:40,666 --> 00:02:43,933
than those who simply accept to what a screen tells them

48
00:02:43,933 --> 00:02:46,500
fourth is digital fluency

49
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:51,000
not just using devices but understanding how information travels

50
00:02:51,100 --> 00:02:53,733
how systems can be manipulated

51
00:02:53,833 --> 00:02:58,866
how to protect privacy and how to evaluate whether something is true

52
00:02:59,100 --> 00:03:03,500
in an era of generated content and deepfakes

53
00:03:03,633 --> 00:03:05,500
this is a survival skill

54
00:03:05,666 --> 00:03:08,966
these four technical capabilities form the foundation

55
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:12,666
but here is what will truly separate your child from the machines

56
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:15,533
because this is where the story gets remarkable

57
00:03:15,666 --> 00:03:20,466
MIT Sloan researchers develop a framework called ipok

58
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:24,400
it identifies five categories of human capability

59
00:03:24,666 --> 00:03:27,666
that artificial intelligence cannot replicate

60
00:03:27,866 --> 00:03:29,866
and here is the extraordinary finding

61
00:03:29,866 --> 00:03:34,900
every single one of these capabilities is associated with employment

62
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:36,933
growth not decline

63
00:03:37,033 --> 00:03:39,000
the first is empathy

64
00:03:39,066 --> 00:03:43,200
the ability to genuinely understand what another person is feeling

65
00:03:43,266 --> 00:03:48,066
to read a room to respond to human emotion with human sensitivity

66
00:03:48,166 --> 00:03:53,733
no language model feels no algorithm grieves or celebrates

67
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,100
empathy is irreplaceable the second is perspective

68
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:00,400
the capacity for ethical judgment

69
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:01,500
moral reasoning

70
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:05,000
and seeing a situation from multiple angles before acting

71
00:04:05,066 --> 00:04:09,733
this is what leaders do this is what doctors do at the bedside

72
00:04:09,933 --> 00:04:12,600
this is what parents do every single day

73
00:04:12,633 --> 00:04:15,600
artificial intelligence can generate options

74
00:04:15,633 --> 00:04:17,300
it cannot carry moral weight

75
00:04:17,433 --> 00:04:19,466
the third is originality

76
00:04:19,966 --> 00:04:24,100
genuine creativity not remixing existing patterns

77
00:04:24,100 --> 00:04:26,466
which is what artificial intelligence does

78
00:04:26,466 --> 00:04:30,333
but generating truly novel ideas from lived experience

79
00:04:30,466 --> 00:04:31,700
imagination

80
00:04:31,766 --> 00:04:36,066
and the kind of intuition that comes from being alive in the world

81
00:04:36,166 --> 00:04:38,466
the greatest artists entrepreneurs

82
00:04:38,466 --> 00:04:41,833
and scientists of the next generation will be valued

83
00:04:41,833 --> 00:04:46,900
precisely because they think in ways machines cannot predict

84
00:04:47,300 --> 00:04:51,200
the fourth is connection the ability to inspire

85
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:55,400
to lead to communicate a vision that makes people want to follow

86
00:04:55,400 --> 00:04:57,666
this is the quality of great teachers

87
00:04:57,766 --> 00:04:59,766
great coaches great founders

88
00:04:59,966 --> 00:05:05,100
it requires trust and trust requires a human being on the other side

89
00:05:05,233 --> 00:05:09,733
the fifth is human drive greet betso perseverance

90
00:05:10,266 --> 00:05:12,866
the ability to push through failure

91
00:05:12,866 --> 00:05:17,066
adapt after disappointment and keep moving when the path disappears

92
00:05:17,866 --> 00:05:21,533
artificial intelligence does not know what it means to struggle

93
00:05:22,033 --> 00:05:25,733
your child shaped by real challenges does

94
00:05:26,433 --> 00:05:31,866
Workday's 2026 research confirms that 83% of business leaders

95
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:35,966
believe artificial intelligence is making human skills more important

96
00:05:35,966 --> 00:05:38,500
not less let that land for a moment

97
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,533
the smarter artificial intelligence gets

98
00:05:41,700 --> 00:05:44,200
the more valuable your humanity becomes

